WEBVTT

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:52.530
Across the globe from Scotland to China with world's greatest train ride videos.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:56.960
Experience the breathtaking thrill and adventure of authentic train travel as you follow the

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.000
tracks to unique history, fascinating people and breath

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:24.880
taking scenery all aboard alexander ivanovich michailov engineer on the trans-siberian

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.480
and his assistant andre are about to take charge of their train

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:49.390
final inspection of the locomotive they receive their instructions

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:58.770
and the keys are handed over to them the train crews all have to wear uniforms

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:22.050
Prior to every trip, there is a medical check-up, an extra measure to ensure transportation safety.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:36.780
The Russian Railways were created in 1836 by an edict of the Tsar.

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:42.380
The first line, a few miles long, was opened a year later near St. Petersburg, the capital at the time.

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:50.860
Since that time, the Russian, then Soviet, Railways has been through tragic cycles of destruction followed by reconstruction.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.970
1917, The Revolution.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:22.700
1919 civil war and foreign intervention the whole infrastructure of the railway is destroyed

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:27.170
lennon would say if the trains stop it's the death of the proletariat

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:30.000
getting the transportation system back up and running

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:52.120
is the number one priority.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:55.040
1941, the German invasion.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.660
40,000 miles of track out of commission.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.670
4,000 stations destroyed.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:23.800
1945 once again it all has to be reconstructed alexander does one last check

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:40.150
Moscow. The Trans-Siberian is a daily train. It leaves from the Yaroslav station.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:13.760
the head of the cave.

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:28.430
Its name is Rossia, the Red.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:00.000
A vast country, more than 40 times the area of France, nearly two and a half times the

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:06.370
USA and a vast railway system, 91,000 miles of lines, a vital network for the country.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:11.290
It handles 45% of the country's passenger traffic, 10 million people a day for a population

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:13.570
of 168 million.

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:16.090
Without the railroad, the Soviet Union would be paralyzed.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:26.050
It handles 65% of the freight transport.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:28.000
We're off on a six-day voyage.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.420
Alexander is 38 years old.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:50.480
He's married and has three daughters.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:52.430
He's a member of the Communist Party.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:54.860
His assistant is 24 years old.

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:08.430
Valentina is also 38.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:10.550
They've been married for 15 years.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.720
On Sunday mornings, there's a free market nearby.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:37.530
We won't be showing you the picture postcard Moscow:

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:41.280
Red Square, the Golden Domes of the Kremlin, the banks of the Moskova,

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:47.090
but everyday Moscow.

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:48.220
Lines.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.440
The curse of daily life for the Soviets.

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.920
Waiting in lines takes up several hours a day.

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:03.880
"Alexander and Valentina take us to their place.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:08.320
This is also something new, being admitted into the privacy of a Soviet home.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:15.930
Will they feel free to speak their minds?"

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:19.390
They live about 20 kilometers from the center of Moscow.

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:30.000
These projects are 20 to 25 stories high.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:44.190
latest housing Moscow has nine million inhabitants they've just moved in they

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:48.830
have 700 square feet for six people father mother the three children and the

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:53.780
grandmother this is the new norm but most Muscovites squeezed into tiny

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:55.880
apartments don't have nearly that much room

00:08:55.880 --> 00:09:00.000
they even have their own kitchen whereas before one kitchen shared by two or

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:08.170
even four apartments was standard. How do they manage? Alexander's take-home pay is

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:11.770
350 rubles a month which makes six hundred and thirty dollars at the

00:09:11.770 --> 00:09:16.530
official exchange rate and Valentina spends 400 rubles a month for food. So

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:22.810
how do they make ends meet? Valentina makes 80 rubles knitting sweaters and the

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:27.620
grandmother's pension is 30 rubles a month so they have 460 rubles coming in

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:30.000
every month but then you have to take out 25 rubles

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.740
for rent, electricity, and the telephone.

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:36.040
So for a family of six that leaves 35 rubles

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:39.090
for clothes, leisure, and health.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:41.090
For even though the local doctor is free,

00:09:41.090 --> 00:09:44.730
the cost of medicine is reimbursed only for serious illnesses.

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:48.220
That means that a car, 9,000 or 10,000 rubles,

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:52.220
and a two-year wait is something they can only dream about.

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:55.060
They had to save for years to buy a TV,

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:57.980
800 rubles for the lowest-priced models.

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:00.000
The electric clock on the television?

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:24.110
Alexander made it himself.

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:27.400
The record player cost 600 pupils.

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:30.000
We should also add that buying on credit doesn't exist.

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:33.540
No, their life isn't easy. Valentina doesn't hide the fact.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:37.740
Perestroika, glasnost, restructuration and transparency.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:39.740
That's all anyone ever talks about.

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:42.430
What are you lacking the most?

00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:46.680
What are we lacking now?

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:48.750
There's a shortage of lots of things.

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:50.740
Yeah, lots of things we're missing.

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:53.790
But what about you? What are you lacking?

00:10:53.790 --> 00:11:00.000
What are we lacking?

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.000
The problem is everyday goods. Often, we have the money, but there's nothing to buy with it.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:08.230
Are there certain things you envy about the women that live in the West?

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:09.860
Certain things, yes.

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:53.360
what specifically do you envy in them as a woman I envy them the

00:11:53.360 --> 00:12:00.000
abundance all those products in the stores food

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:12.430
They can buy clothes, and they're free to travel with their children, with their families.

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:16.740
You're not talking about traveling within the country, but trips abroad.

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:23.260
Yes, they can take trips abroad with their families, they travel.

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:29.960
And for that I do envy them.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:31.890
I do like living in the USSR.

00:13:01.690 --> 00:13:09.290
Well, I want us to be able to speak out, to express our ideas without being judged or

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:22.230
persecuted. Freedom of speech, freedom of expression, is something quite wonderful.

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:26.670
Here's one of the rare visible effects of the perestroika: Arbat Street in Moscow.

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:30.000
It's one of the main arteries of the old town. Napoleon's troops used it

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:45.790
it to reach the Kremlin. Today it's a pedestrian street. This is something new. Painters of

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:50.680
all sorts have fled the stodgy academies and taken to the streets. Here they exhibit and

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:55.250
sell their work. Before Gorbachev, Arbat Street was just like any other street.

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:10.690
It's become a strip of freedom, the envy of all the rest of the Soviet Union.

00:14:10.690 --> 00:14:21.790
How long will it take to really change things, and how long will the perestroika last?

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:23.650
That all depends on how we go about it.

00:14:23.650 --> 00:14:29.090
It took Lenin a long time to found the party and bring down the old order.

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:32.270
It's hard to say.

00:14:35.050 --> 00:14:36.570
It won't be all that quick.

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:40.500
Everyone has to feel it from within.

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:48.280
I think our children will feel the effects of the perestroika.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:53.400
One last question, Valya.

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:57.110
You're wearing a cross and you have an icon of the Virgin Mary in your home.

00:14:57.210 --> 00:14:59.030
Are you religious? Do you believe in God?

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:05.970
I don't believe in an actual being who exists somewhere up in heaven.

00:15:05.970 --> 00:15:12.830
For me, God is generosity, human kindness, that which is holy in the human soul.

00:15:12.830 --> 00:15:20.790
That's what God is for me.

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:27.770
Back on the Trans-Siberian.

00:15:27.770 --> 00:15:29.920
Alexander works 41 hours a week.

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:34.130
He gets a month's vacation, he'll retire at the age of 51.

00:15:34.130 --> 00:15:38.910
The railway company gives him and his family one free trip per year.

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:42.750
There are two types of car, with two or four sleeping berths.

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:49.890
Shared sinks, no showers, and that's a problem for the six-day travelers, those going all the way to Vladivostok.

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:56.950
This couple lives in Sheetap in Siberia.

00:15:56.950 --> 00:16:00.000
The old woman just spent 15 days in Moscow visiting her children.

00:16:02.140 --> 00:16:16.190
You're finally settled in and already you've come to the realization that the Trans-Siberian of your dreams with its padded seats, lacquered furniture in the compartments, the dining cars alight with dazzling crystal and silverware is a thing of the past.

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:18.490
You'll come to know a different train.

00:16:39.280 --> 00:16:45.990
5810 miles the longest train route in the world more than a quarter of the way around the earth

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:52.800
for train lovers it's a legend a myth the journey lasts six days by plane it takes eight hours

00:16:53.630 --> 00:16:57.200
noon in moscow means 7 p.m in vladivostok

00:17:12.440 --> 00:17:15.440
all the trains are in radio contact with the way stations,

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:21.050
which link up with the major stations.

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:30.000
The price of it to get

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.160
Moscow, Vladivostok, is 69 rubles, about $130,

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:36.310
in a compartment with four berths.

00:17:36.310 --> 00:17:38.360
It's $290 for a double.

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:04.160
- What is it? - A sofa, a suitcase.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:08.900
- A sofa, a suitcase, everything is fine.

00:18:50.890 --> 00:18:55.580
The sun has managed to come out from behind the clouds to greet us as we pull into Yaroslav,

00:18:55.580 --> 00:18:57.590
one of the most beautiful cities of Russia.

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:04.980
Yaroslav was founded in the year 1010, even before Moscow,

00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:10.220
as a citadel to control the crossings of the Volga River, a vital trade route.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:15.560
Down through the centuries, the merchants have built more than 50 churches.

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:08.970
In the forest near Yaroslav, we have a curious establishment.

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:12.260
It's not a military training camp, and it's not a vacation village.

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:15.430
It's more like an amusement park ride, a scale model toy,

00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:20.220
but it also seems like very cleverly organized indoctrination.

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:23.890
These are not all children of railway employees,

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:26.470
but they'll all mostly end up working for the company.

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:40.310
Today, our station master is a girl.

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:42.940
on our way again. The track gauge is 1 meter 52, larger than in the rest of Europe where

00:21:42.940 --> 00:21:48.920
it's 1 meter 43. And another piece of information as we're crossing the Volga, this train we're

00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:54.250
riding in, the Trans-Siberian, has an average speed of 36 and a half miles per hour. And

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:58.910
Glasnost has made it known that upping the speed is out of the question before the year 2000.

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:05.330
The Moscow Leningrad Express, which tops 125 miles per hour, will have no rivals.

00:22:06.070 --> 00:22:09.270
The fact is, the Soviet railways has other pressing needs.

00:22:09.650 --> 00:22:27.470
New track, extending lines, renewal of the rolling stock, the samovar in every car,

00:22:27.470 --> 00:22:30.000
a cup of tea with sugar, costs.

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:36.800
a penny a bit less without sugar there are two attendants per car they take turns each working

00:22:36.800 --> 00:22:52.540
one 12-hour shift per day 38 attendants for the 19 cars that make up the train two dutch tourists

00:22:52.540 --> 00:22:58.090
saraya karam and elvira helmskerk they're going to vacation in china and rather than

00:22:58.090 --> 00:23:00.000
take the train they've succumbed to the lure

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:13.080
of the Trans-Siberian.

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:15.820
And after Irkutsk, where are you headed?

00:23:15.820 --> 00:23:19.590
After three days, we head for Peking.

00:23:19.590 --> 00:23:22.470
Yes, Peking.

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:24.930
Then again by train to Sihan.

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:28.000
After that, the plane to Chengdu.

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:38.320
then we go to Gilin. The nature is wonderful at Gilin. Then we fly to Hong Kong. What do you think

00:23:38.320 --> 00:23:45.690
of these cars on the Trans-Siberian? The comfort, the personnel. Oh, the service is very good. The

00:23:45.690 --> 00:24:00.000
girls are very nice, but it's a bit hot. The food is good and it's not very expensive. So we have a lot of money to spend on, well,

00:24:00.410 --> 00:24:05.000
We have a lot of money left.

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:09.080
From the second day, and especially the third day out, the towns and villages get further

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:10.520
and further apart.

00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:18.830
We move through stands of beech trees and evergreens, interspersed with clearings.

00:24:18.830 --> 00:24:20.450
The head conductor.

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:23.270
The attendants are under her orders, and they check the tickets.

00:24:23.270 --> 00:24:27.890
There are no ticket takers.

00:24:27.890 --> 00:24:30.000
She announces the stations.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:44.310
He plays the role of disc jockey, selecting the music for the train, three cooks, and a dishwasher.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:03.240
goes door-to-door through the train offering hot dishes.

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:11.010
Sorry, no sandwiches, peanuts, or candy bars.

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:50.140
There are two identical dining cars in the middle of the train,

00:25:50.140 --> 00:25:53.160
open from nine in the morning till nine at night.

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:56.160
They close for two hours in the afternoon.

00:25:56.160 --> 00:25:59.060
You can get a good hearty meal for about $3.

00:26:00.030 --> 00:26:04.610
Wine, beer, and liquor are not allowed anywhere in the Soviet railway system.

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:16.130
The hors d'oeuvres are sold by weight, not by the portion.

00:26:17.200 --> 00:26:19.700
Each slice of sausage is carefully weighed.

00:26:30.700 --> 00:26:37.510
No calculator, but the good old-fashioned abacus.

00:26:37.510 --> 00:27:00.000
Important landmark: we are now leaving Europe and entering Asia.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:02.220
from the Komosol, the communist youth movement.

00:27:02.790 --> 00:27:03.610
They're quite excited.

00:27:04.090 --> 00:27:04.590
They're giggling.

00:27:05.540 --> 00:27:06.240
What's going on?

00:27:11.440 --> 00:27:13.500
Well, we're students in a polytechnic school.

00:27:14.240 --> 00:27:16.800
We were sent to Sochi on an agricultural assignment.

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:19.510
We were picking walnuts and tea.

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:24.530
Now we're going back to Irkutsk via Moscow.

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:29.020
And at Novosibirsk, we missed our train.

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:35.410
We almost had to walk home.

00:27:35.410 --> 00:27:38.870
They let us get on this train which will take us to the taiga.

00:27:38.870 --> 00:27:49.350
We just hope we can catch up with our train.

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:52.720
The next stop, the moment of truth for these girls.

00:28:08.090 --> 00:28:20.170
Rain, sleet, and dust have all left the cars covered with a thick layer of dirt.

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:25.220
There's no regular cleaning service, so if you want a clean window, it's a do-it-yourself proposition.

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:39.730
Boredom has set in, and each traveler plunges into his own thoughts, rocked by a melancholy air.

00:29:25.850 --> 00:29:30.000
perceptibly night follows day which follows night. Is today Tuesday?

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.020
or Thursday. Little by little

00:29:32.020 --> 00:29:33.540
we've lost the notion of time.

00:29:34.450 --> 00:29:35.470
Three o'clock in the morning.

00:29:36.320 --> 00:29:38.420
One more station just like all the others.

00:29:39.200 --> 00:29:39.980
Where are we?

00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:42.820
Numbed by the rhythm of the rails, it's been a long

00:29:42.820 --> 00:29:44.920
time since we've even glanced at the map.

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:18.530
Work continues: the mail, servicemen getting back to their units from leave.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:37.380
we're about to get moving nope false alarm the train is immobilized for more than two hours

00:30:37.910 --> 00:30:42.250
it's an emergency the wheels of the car in front of us were getting dangerously hot

00:30:42.250 --> 00:30:52.660
they have to change the whole wheel assembly just how close was it to breaking was this a near

00:30:52.660 --> 00:31:00.000
disaster the train crew is not saying you might think you're up against the usual soviet smokescreen

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:04.950
of secrecy. Well, things have changed. This extraordinary film you're about to see was

00:31:04.950 --> 00:31:16.430
given to us by the Minister of Communications. It's the Minister Nikolai Khodaryov speaking.

00:31:16.430 --> 00:31:23.090
When will all the words be backed up by action? Do you fully realize your responsibilities

00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:30.000
to the public and to your superiors? What kind of political and national awareness do

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:39.630
you have. Yes, it is possible to go through a bad quarter, even a second bad quarter,

00:31:39.630 --> 00:31:44.430
but when the commitments are never respected, it means you couldn't care less about your

00:31:44.430 --> 00:31:52.400
responsibilities and your work. The narrator continues: What you see here are not the ravages

00:31:52.400 --> 00:32:00.000
of war, but today's vandalism. This is how the users treat the cars. The country badly needs

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:05.560
rolling stock, and we can no longer say that this is simply due to negligence. Putting

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:22.110
equipment into service and repairing it are costly operations.

00:32:22.110 --> 00:32:26.960
The commentator also criticizes the computerized system of ticket reservations and sales.

00:32:26.960 --> 00:32:30.000
There's only one center in Moscow for a population of none.

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:36.700
million. The employees are not always efficient, he specifies. He also points

00:32:36.700 --> 00:32:43.640
out the shortage of telephones, the shortage of trains. The system doesn't

00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:57.510
measure up, he concludes. Boris Tikmelyov, doctor of science, talks about

00:32:57.510 --> 00:32:59.700
development of locomotive prototypes.

00:33:00.280 --> 00:33:02.540
We've lost sight of the national priority.

00:33:02.540 --> 00:33:05.660
In France, they started this kind of research ten years after us,

00:33:05.660 --> 00:33:07.440
but they've already concluded.

00:33:07.440 --> 00:33:10.020
They've decided to launch a series of locomotives

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:12.890
with thyristor-controlled connectorless motors.

00:33:12.890 --> 00:33:17.060
Forty-four locomotives are already under construction.

00:33:17.060 --> 00:33:21.890
- Where? - In France, of course.

00:33:21.890 --> 00:33:24.640
- And here? - And here it's still dragging along.

00:33:30.030 --> 00:33:58.130
My soul is free to breathe, I love my land, my beloved land, my beloved song.

00:34:02.360 --> 00:34:08.780
Novosibirsk, the third largest city after Moscow and Leningrad, a population of 1,400,000.

00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:11.580
This mushroom town has doubled in 20 years.

00:34:11.960 --> 00:34:14.440
It's the jewel of the new dynamic Siberia.

00:35:16.250 --> 00:35:20.930
The daycare center of the station is resolutely placed under the edifying protection of Comrade

00:35:20.930 --> 00:35:30.000
Lennon. We see him as a blonde, curly-haired baby, then a little boy, an adolescent, a young

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:37.400
man, as an adult, and lastly with a protective arm around his mother.

00:36:02.010 --> 00:36:16.860
Siberia has a higher standard of living than the rest of the country.

00:36:16.860 --> 00:36:21.640
Thirty-nine-year-old Sergei Finotko, engineer on the Trans-Siberian, owns a car, unlike

00:36:21.640 --> 00:36:23.560
his co-workers in Moscow.

00:36:23.560 --> 00:36:26.280
The fact is, he earns 15% more than them.

00:36:26.280 --> 00:36:30.000
If he were to live even further out, in the Soviet Far East, his salary would be

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:38.580
30% more and in Kamchatka 100% more. A strong incentive. The idea is to populate these almost

00:36:38.580 --> 00:36:39.820
uninhabited territories.

00:37:00.540 --> 00:37:05.840
Sergei and his family also own a dacha, a small country house, with a vegetable garden.

00:37:05.960 --> 00:37:12.660
They built it themselves, just like their neighbors building his. The whole village, in fact, was built by the inhabitants.

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:30.000
They don't own the land. It's on loan.

00:37:30.030 --> 00:37:34.080
from the state but they will be able to pass the house on to their children. It

00:37:34.080 --> 00:37:38.340
was Alexis, Sergei's father-in-law, who obtained this benefit as a veteran.

00:37:38.340 --> 00:38:00.000
"There was only a one-year wait," he says. Sergei, the locomotive engineer,

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:05.810
engineer likes living in Siberia. Obviously, he enjoys being able to fish in these unpolluted

00:38:05.810 --> 00:38:11.840
rivers that he has all to himself, but there's more. There are the wide open spaces which

00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:13.760
give a feeling of limitless freedom.

00:38:31.340 --> 00:38:35.180
Bring me closer, I have to clean it up.

00:38:35.180 --> 00:38:37.870
- Are you waiting for me? - Yes, yes, yes.

00:38:37.870 --> 00:38:41.070
- The controller is coming. Take care. - Well done, well done.

00:38:41.070 --> 00:38:42.740
- Take care.

00:38:42.740 --> 00:38:45.650
- Mityusha, about the controller there. - Yes, come on, come on.

00:38:45.650 --> 00:38:50.680
- So, this must be sorted immediately, because in the first place, you say, and what in the second?

00:38:50.680 --> 00:38:54.420
- Well, let's. This one will go to fry, it's already ready.

00:38:54.420 --> 00:38:55.920
- That's it, yes, the controller.

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:15.550
they talk about Perestroika here too. Do you have co-workers who are against Perestroika?

00:39:15.550 --> 00:39:20.150
Yes, and not only co-workers, I have friends unfortunately who are against it. If it weren't

00:39:20.150 --> 00:39:30.000
for them, the goals would already have been reached. Why are they against Perestroika?

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:35.150
opponents and then there are the soft opponents. The soft opponents are not

00:39:35.150 --> 00:39:41.510
actively resisting but they do slow down the perestroika. People don't all think

00:39:41.510 --> 00:39:45.410
these things out at the same pace. We hope the perestroika will pick up

00:39:45.410 --> 00:39:51.350
momentum. Among your co-workers is there an active resistance or maybe even

00:39:51.350 --> 00:40:00.000
sabotage? No, no sabotage but more of an inertia. Not so much a physical inertia

00:40:00.030 --> 00:40:06.850
inertia, it's more of a moral inertia. It's hard to change people's everyday habits.

00:40:06.850 --> 00:40:13.290
That's true. But there are also people who don't like to work, and they drag their heels

00:40:13.290 --> 00:40:23.960
on perestroika. The vegetable garden is their pride and joy.

00:40:23.960 --> 00:40:30.000
It doesn't mean that much less time waiting in the food store lines.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:53.810
Sergei has started his shift.

00:40:53.810 --> 00:40:57.140
Every major station brings a new crop of local newspapers.

00:41:34.730 --> 00:41:42.390
5-2-8-1-6-2

00:42:18.040 --> 00:42:20.090
We're changing locomotives.

00:42:20.090 --> 00:42:21.630
The Soviets designed them

00:42:21.630 --> 00:42:23.570
and they're manufactured in Czechoslovakia

00:42:23.570 --> 00:42:25.500
in the framework of the Kamekom,

00:42:25.500 --> 00:42:27.560
the Eastern Bloc Economic Alliance.

00:42:32.620 --> 00:42:42.870
Of the 5,810 miles of the Trans-Siberian, 3,900 miles are electrified.

00:42:42.870 --> 00:42:45.040
Diesel power covers the rest.

00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:49.590
Steam power was finally phased out on the whole network only in 1978.

00:44:12.540 --> 00:44:15.950
We begin to see snow as we approach Lake Baikal.

00:44:15.950 --> 00:44:24.380
The Trans-Siberian follows its shoreline for 170 miles.

00:44:24.380 --> 00:44:30.000
This is the deepest lake in the world, just over a mile deep.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:48.610
one-fifth of our planet's fresh water created 25 million years ago and of the

00:44:48.610 --> 00:44:53.010
dozens of species of fish it contains 27 are found nowhere else

00:45:45.200 --> 00:45:51.890
we find the same charm of the old timber work stations in the Izbaz of Irkutsk near Baikal.

00:45:51.890 --> 00:46:00.000
Like this one, the home of Prince Trubetskoy, leader of the December uprising. On the 14th

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:05.080
A group of aristocrats rebelled in order to impose a constitutional monarchy on the Tsar.

00:46:05.080 --> 00:46:09.640
The rebellion was crushed and 120 Decembrists were exiled to Siberia.

00:46:09.640 --> 00:46:15.490
Trubetskoy stayed for 30 years.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:05.480
was hostess to a renowned literary salon at Irkutsk at that time French was

00:47:05.480 --> 00:47:10.050
the language used every day by the cultured class the Soviet rulers regard

00:47:10.050 --> 00:47:13.890
the Decembrists as true revolutionaries and they honor their memory

00:47:32.300 --> 00:47:36.360
In this region, they've succeeded in creating a second Trans-Siberian Line,

00:47:36.360 --> 00:47:38.560
2,600 miles long.

00:47:38.560 --> 00:47:40.480
It took more than 45 years.

00:47:40.480 --> 00:47:43.360
The work was often interrupted, but always resumed,

00:47:43.360 --> 00:47:47.120
and all under some of the worst geographic and climatic conditions on Earth.

00:47:47.120 --> 00:47:50.390
Much more than a public works project, it was an epic.

00:47:50.390 --> 00:47:53.120
It's the BAM, the Baikal Amur Line,

00:47:53.120 --> 00:47:55.630
named after the mighty river of the Far East.

00:47:55.630 --> 00:48:00.000
It passes to the north of Lake Baikal.

00:48:00.160 --> 00:48:10.390
On the BAM line, we dug seven tunnels, constructed more than 50 stations, seven mountain ranges,

00:48:10.390 --> 00:48:21.340
the permafrost, seismic problems, a very dense system of rivers.

00:48:21.340 --> 00:48:30.000
The thermometer sometimes drops to minus 76 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:52.630
1984 the line was opened the BAM opens up enormous possibilities for

00:48:52.630 --> 00:48:58.480
transportation and industry between Moscow and Vladivostok

00:49:01.310 --> 00:49:03.360
back underway with our Trans-Siberian.

00:49:33.100 --> 00:49:38.120
The region of Khabarovsk is included in the vast project to develop the Soviet Far East

00:49:38.120 --> 00:49:41.620
and create a practically autonomous industrial and technical entity.

00:49:41.620 --> 00:49:45.130
250 new enterprises have been set up.

00:49:45.130 --> 00:49:48.530
The salaries here are 30% higher than in Moscow.

00:50:02.940 --> 00:50:17.640
The Amur, a vast river, a natural frontier between Russia and China, the station of Khabarovsk.

00:50:30.160 --> 00:50:52.180
The mountains we see across the horizon are in China.

00:51:03.320 --> 00:51:05.930
Up until now, we've been traveling east.

00:51:05.930 --> 00:51:12.320
Now we head due south.

00:51:12.320 --> 00:51:17.130
The chess players have finally finished their interminable game.

00:51:17.130 --> 00:51:21.240
In the USSR, they like formal ceremonies.

00:51:21.240 --> 00:51:30.000
They're also fond of medals, so this is a perfect occasion.

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:43.090
Nacobka, end of the line.

00:51:43.810 --> 00:51:45.890
Foreigners can't go all the way to Vladivostok.

00:51:45.890 --> 00:51:47.350
They have to get off here.

00:52:04.920 --> 00:52:06.670
Well, there we have it.

00:52:06.890 --> 00:52:07.690
The trip is finished.

00:52:08.440 --> 00:52:10.360
The longest train journey in the world.

00:52:10.690 --> 00:52:12.410
5,810 miles.

00:52:12.890 --> 00:52:14.410
Six days and six nights.

00:52:21.190 --> 00:52:30.000
For those who wanted to live out their dream of the Trans-Siberian Railroad, to exercise

